<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Engelbert I of Nassau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–1442</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Engelbert I, Count of Nassau-Dillenburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Engelbert I was Count of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> and Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>. In 1403 he married Johanna van Polanen, heiress of Breda, which gave the Nassau family large estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and began the line that led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>. He served the dukes of Brabant and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> as a councillor and commander.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/engelbert-i-of-nassau.jpg" alt="Engelbert I of Nassau" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renée Kools · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grote_Kerk_Breda_grafmonument_Engelbrecht_I_van_Nassau_3_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
